M3-c193 L05 None 
  F: When is a real symmetric matrix A positive definite? 
  B: When x^T A x > 0 for every nonzero x in R^n (Definition 1.4). 
  LESSON: Definition 1.4). Under Assumptions F1 to F4 of Lesson 4, the eigenvalue test of Theorem 2.1 turns that property into a computable check. The test needs care: a computed eigenvalue of a singular matrix
M3-c164 L09 C 
  F: In Lab 1, what are the distances for the first seeded matrix with a smallest eigenvalue below -1e-9 (index 0) to the clip-and-rescale matrix R and to the alternating-projection output W? 
  B: 0.175527 to R and 0.163280 to W (Lab 1 output, one floating-point run). 
  LESSON: Lab 1 finds an alternating-projection output that is a correlation matrix within tolerance and closer to the input than the clip-and-rescale matrix. 1 Correlation matrices and the Frobenius norm Throu
M3-c054 L03 T 
  F: For a real n x n matrix A and a real number lambda, how is dim E_lambda found from the rank of A - lambda I? 
  B: dim E_lambda = n minus rank(A - lambda I). E_lambda is the null space of A - lambda I (Definition 1.2), and the null space of an n-column matrix has dimension n minus its rank (Lesson 1, Proposition 3.12). 
  LESSON: 
M3-c083 L05 T 
  F: Is every Gram matrix B^T B of a real m x n matrix symmetric and positive semidefinite? 
  B: Yes, for any m and n at least 1. Its form is x^T B^T B x = ||Bx||^2 >= 0 (Proposition 1.5). 
  LESSON: 
M3-c095 L05 C 
  F: In Lab 1 Part B, how many of the 360 equally spaced angles give a negative form on the unit circle for [[1, 2], [2, 1]]? 
  B: 118 of 360 (Lab 1 output). This is a count on the grid, not the measure of the negative directions, which is one third of the circle (Problem E2). 
  LESSON: Lab 1 shows how a tolerance decides the verdict. The matrices \(B^{\top}B\) are the standard examples, because their form is the squared length \(\|Bx\|^2\), and Proposition 2.4, under Assumptions F1 
M3-c129 L07 T 
  F: Misconception: every projection matrix has an inverse, since P^2 = P. True or false? 
  B: False. P = diag(1, 0) satisfies P^2 = P and has no inverse. If P were invertible, P^2 = P would give P = I, which is false for that P. 
  LESSON: 
M3-c154 L09 T 
  F: For a real symmetric A, what does clipping at epsilon >= 0 produce? 
  B: Assuming F1 to F4 of Lesson 4, a symmetric positive semidefinite matrix with eigenvalues max(lambda_i, epsilon) (Proposition 3.2(a)). The proof writes x^T A_epsilon x as the sum of max(lambda_i, epsilon) y_i^2 with y = Q^T x. 
  LESSON: 
M3-c006 L01 T 
  F: In R^3, for which vectors x is x in span(e1, e2)? 
  B: Exactly those with third coordinate x3 = 0, because every combination c1 e1 + c2 e2 has third coordinate 0 and every vector (a, b, 0) equals a e1 + b e2 (Example 2.3). 
  LESSON: 
M3-c059 L03 C 
  F: In Lab 1, do numpy's eigenpairs of the Example 2.5 matrix have residual below 1e-12? 
  B: Yes, the lab prints True for this check. It prints verdicts and rounded values only, never raw residuals (Lab 1 output). 
