M3-c100 L05 None 
  F: Misconception: a computed eigenvalue of -10^-16 proves the matrix is not positive semidefinite. True or false? 
  B: False. The exact eigenvalue of G in Remark 3.2 is 0, and a floating-point solver may return a value near zero with either sign. Definition 3.1 uses a tolerance for that reason. 
  LESSON: Remark 3.2 (a rank-deficient Gram matrix) Let \(B = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 1 \\ 0 & 1 & 1 \end{pmatrix}\) and \(G = B^{\top}B = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 1 \\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\ 1 & 1 & 2 \end{pmatrix}\). Ex
M3-c002 L01 T 
  F: In a real vector space, what are 0v and (-1)v? 
  B: 0v is the zero vector, and (-1)v equals -v, the additive inverse of v (Lemma 1.3). 
  LESSON: 
M3-c169 L01 T 
  F: Do all echelon forms of a real matrix A have the same number of pivots? 
  B: Yes. Every echelon form of A, obtained by the row operations of Definition 3.3, has the same number of pivots, so the rank is well defined (Proposition 3.12). 
