M3-c119 L07 T 
  F: When the columns of A are independent, does P = A (A^T A)^{-1} A^T send every y to its orthogonal projection onto the column space? 
  B: Assuming F1 to F4 of Lesson 4, yes: P y is the orthogonal projection of y onto the column space for every y (Theorem 1.2(c)). 
  LESSON: 
M3-c097 L05 C 
  F: In Lab 1 Part D, how many eigenvalues of the rank 2 Gram matrix are within 1e-10 of zero? 
  B: One, the exact zero eigenvalue of Remark 3.2 (Lab 1 output). The printed eigenvalues are 0.0000, 1.0000 and 3.0000. 
  LESSON: Remark 3.2 (a rank-deficient Gram matrix) Let \(B = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 1 \\ 0 & 1 & 1 \end{pmatrix}\) and \(G = B^{\top}B = \begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 & 1 \\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\ 1 & 1 & 2 \end{pmatrix}\). Ex
M3-c008 L01 None 
  F: What is a basis of a vector space V, and what is its dimension? 
  B: A basis is a linearly independent list that spans V. If V has a basis of finitely many vectors, its dimension is the number of vectors in such a basis (Definition 3.2). 
  LESSON: 
